 ## Background
-The concept of using attributes beyond simple intensity to guide volume rendering classification has evolved to address the ambiguity of overlapping data ranges. Early work introduced multi-dimensional transfer functions (MDTFs) that incorporated domain knowledge attributes like range weight and local variance to complement gradient-based measures [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent developments applied these principles to specific data types, such as high-resolution color cryo-imaging, where color-based feature detectors (e.g., normalized channel ratios) were developed to highlight specific tissues [S2]. The field has since expanded to include automated graph-based material deduction, shape-based skeletonization, and machine learning approaches to handle high-dimensional and complex volumetric structures [S1, unofficial].
